<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  The roots a content:// URI may be minted for. Deliberately NOT the whole of filesDir.

  Everything this app shares is a keystore or a PGP key: the four producers are the keystore
  share (KeystoreDetailsViewModel), the public-key share and the guarded private-key export
  (PgpKeyDetailsViewModel), and the export of a version an inbound sync displaced
  (PreservedCopiesViewModel). Nothing else calls shareFile.

  That fourth one needs no new root and must not be given one: sync keeps displaced versions in a
  conflict store beside the artifact directory it belongs to — pgp/<user>.conflicts/ and
  keystore/<user>.conflicts/ — which is already inside the two roots named below.

  A wider root than that is not merely untidy. AndroidSettingsService treats
  FileProvider.getUriForFile as a security boundary — it catches the throw and logs "path is
  outside the FileProvider root" — and with a root of "." that check can never fire for anything
  the app itself wrote. Naming only the two directories that are genuinely shareable is what
  makes that guard real, and it puts the vault database, the device keyring (keyring.pmk), the
  portable-recovery material and the staged sync temp files permanently out of reach of a
  content URI, whatever a future call site asks for.

  Files picked by the user are staged into cacheDir by AndroidFileHandler and are already
  outside these roots, so the import path does not depend on any of this.
-->
<paths>
    <files-path name="keystore" path="keystore/" />
    <files-path name="pgp" path="pgp/" />
</paths>
